1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:05,041
At the end of Chapter 9, Asha's report
still trusted every answer.

2
00:00:05,061 --> 00:00:10,070
Before she builds a game, she checks what
one guess does with a word.

3
00:00:10,100 --> 00:00:12,528
Here is the problem, in lines 2 and 3.

4
00:00:12,548 --> 00:00:16,781
Line 3 asks for a guess, and converts the
answer with int at once.

5
00:00:16,801 --> 00:00:18,843
A customer types the word seven.

6
00:00:30,213 --> 00:00:32,306
It crashes, with a Value Error.

7
00:00:32,326 --> 00:00:34,488
You met this error in Chapter 4.

8
00:00:34,508 --> 00:00:38,373
Int can only convert text that looks like
a whole number.

9
00:00:38,400 --> 00:00:44,082
Today, Asha builds a game that handles a
word, an empty answer or a wrong number,

10
00:00:44,102 --> 00:00:45,279
without crashing.

11
00:00:45,887 --> 00:00:48,947
Welcome to Chapter 10, the second boss
level!

12
00:00:48,967 --> 00:00:53,477
You will combine decisions, loops and
careful checks into one game.

13
00:00:53,500 --> 00:00:57,902
And an AI assistant will promise you a
number it can never pick.

14
00:00:57,922 --> 00:00:59,353
A test will prove it.

15
00:00:59,950 --> 00:01:04,206
The Corner Cafe has a glass jar of
biscuits on the counter.

16
00:01:04,226 --> 00:01:05,800
It holds from 1 to 20.

17
00:01:05,833 --> 00:01:10,026
Each customer gets five guesses, with a
hint after each one.

18
00:01:10,046 --> 00:01:12,553
Guess right, and you win a free tea.

19
00:01:12,600 --> 00:01:15,763
And this time, no answer may crash the
game.

20
00:01:15,783 --> 00:01:20,247
Not a word, not an empty answer, not a
number outside 1 to 20.

21
00:01:20,853 --> 00:01:26,338
You already know almost every piece:
decisions from Chapter 6, and a while loop

22
00:01:26,358 --> 00:01:27,383
from Chapter 7.

23
00:01:27,433 --> 00:01:31,143
A for loop from Chapter 8, and testing
from Chapter 9.

24
00:01:31,167 --> 00:01:36,494
And two new tools: the random module, to
pick the secret number, and is digit, to

25
00:01:36,514 --> 00:01:37,550
check an answer.

26
00:01:38,150 --> 00:01:40,147
Here is where we are going.

27
00:01:40,167 --> 00:01:44,480
Look at the game on the right: the header,
with the rules.

28
00:01:44,500 --> 00:01:45,770
The player guesses 12.

29
00:01:45,790 --> 00:01:47,295
Too high, four tries left.

30
00:01:47,315 --> 00:01:47,705
Then 3.

31
00:01:47,725 --> 00:01:49,230
Too low, three tries left.

32
00:01:49,267 --> 00:01:49,875
Then 7.

33
00:01:49,895 --> 00:01:50,594
Correct!

34
00:01:50,614 --> 00:01:53,737
Three guesses used, and a free tea.

35
00:01:53,767 --> 00:01:55,327
About twenty lines of code.

36
00:01:55,347 --> 00:01:58,605
By the end of this video, you will
understand every one.

37
00:01:59,183 --> 00:02:01,414
Asha writes down what done means first.

38
00:02:01,434 --> 00:02:03,953
The game is finished when all five are
true.

39
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:04,247
One.

40
00:02:04,267 --> 00:02:08,457
It picks a secret from 1 to 20, and every
number can be picked.

41
00:02:08,477 --> 00:02:09,660
Remember that one!

42
00:02:09,700 --> 00:02:10,515
Two and three.

43
00:02:10,535 --> 00:02:14,570
Hints after each guess, and a clear end
after a win or five guesses.

44
00:02:14,600 --> 00:02:14,913
Four.

45
00:02:14,933 --> 00:02:19,445
A word, an empty answer, or a number
outside 1 to 20 gets a message.

46
00:02:19,465 --> 00:02:23,310
It does not count as a try, and it never
crashes the game.

47
00:02:23,333 --> 00:02:23,926
And five.

48
00:02:23,946 --> 00:02:27,191
It passes at least six tests with known
results.

49
00:02:27,787 --> 00:02:30,089
Quick check, using criterion four.

50
00:02:30,109 --> 00:02:33,164
Which answer counts as one of the five
tries?

51
00:02:44,547 --> 00:02:45,012
D, 12.

52
00:02:45,032 --> 00:02:47,759
It is a whole number from 1 to 20.

53
00:02:47,779 --> 00:02:53,820
A word, an empty answer and 25 get a
message, and the player keeps the try.

54
00:02:54,420 --> 00:02:55,441
Take a breather.

55
00:02:55,461 --> 00:02:57,980
You know the game, and what done means.

56
00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:02,933
Next, Asha plans the rules with an AI
assistant, before any code.

57
00:03:07,683 --> 00:03:13,265
In this project, all six AI stages work on
one game, turn by turn as Asha builds it.

58
00:03:13,285 --> 00:03:14,199
Stage 1: plan.

59
00:03:14,219 --> 00:03:17,199
Asha says what she knows, and what she
wants.

60
00:03:17,219 --> 00:03:18,933
And she says: no code yet.

61
00:03:18,967 --> 00:03:24,413
The AI lists the values to remember: the
secret, the tries, and whether the player

62
00:03:24,433 --> 00:03:24,947
has won.

63
00:03:24,967 --> 00:03:27,347
Then a rule for low, high and equal.

64
00:03:27,367 --> 00:03:29,591
It is a good plan for numbers.

65
00:03:29,611 --> 00:03:32,808
But what about a customer who types a
word?

66
00:03:33,420 --> 00:03:36,734
Asha plays the game on paper, and types a
word.

67
00:03:36,754 --> 00:03:38,720
The plan has no rule for it.

68
00:03:38,767 --> 00:03:39,962
So she adds a rule.

69
00:03:39,982 --> 00:03:43,287
An answer that is not a whole number gets
a message.

70
00:03:43,333 --> 00:03:44,765
And a second rule.

71
00:03:44,785 --> 00:03:48,313
A number outside 1 to 20 gets a message
too.

72
00:03:48,333 --> 00:03:50,680
Neither one costs the player a try.

73
00:03:50,700 --> 00:03:53,656
A typing mistake should not lose a free
tea!

74
00:03:54,250 --> 00:03:55,114
Time to build.

75
00:03:55,134 --> 00:04:00,290
Step 1 starts with a fixed secret, 7, so
Asha knows what every guess should print.

76
00:04:00,333 --> 00:04:03,513
Line 3 asks for one guess and converts it.

77
00:04:03,533 --> 00:04:07,513
Lines 4 and 5: if the guess equals the
secret, Correct.

78
00:04:07,533 --> 00:04:11,083
Lines 6 and 7: if it is lower, Too low.

79
00:04:11,133 --> 00:04:15,126
And lines 8 and 9: anything else is
higher, so Too high.

80
00:04:26,480 --> 00:04:27,154
Too high!

81
00:04:27,174 --> 00:04:31,857
12 is not equal to 7, and not lower, so
the else branch runs.

82
00:04:32,453 --> 00:04:33,521
Stage 2: write.

83
00:04:33,541 --> 00:04:35,986
Asha wrote these branches herself.

84
00:04:36,006 --> 00:04:41,496
She asks the AI which message each guess
gets, below, equal to and above the

85
00:04:41,516 --> 00:04:42,003
secret.

86
00:04:42,033 --> 00:04:44,970
The AI names one message for each guess.

87
00:04:44,990 --> 00:04:46,743
Every branch can happen.

88
00:04:46,767 --> 00:04:51,494
She runs her code with 12, and gets the
AI's answer: Too high.

89
00:04:52,083 --> 00:04:53,701
Step 2: five tries.

90
00:04:53,721 --> 00:04:57,063
Line 3 counts the tries, starting at 0.

91
00:04:57,100 --> 00:04:59,969
Line 4 repeats while tries is less than 5.

92
00:04:59,989 --> 00:05:03,820
That is true for 0, 1, 2, 3 and 4: exactly
five guesses.

93
00:05:03,867 --> 00:05:07,787
Line 6 adds one try after each guess.

94
00:05:07,833 --> 00:05:13,381
When the guess is right, line 8 prints
Correct with the tries used, and line 9

95
00:05:13,401 --> 00:05:15,023
breaks out of the loop.

96
00:05:15,067 --> 00:05:21,875
The hints on lines 11 and 13 also say how
many tries are left: 5, minus the tries

97
00:05:21,895 --> 00:05:22,297
used.

98
00:05:22,333 --> 00:05:25,886
And after the loop, line 14 checks the
last guess.

99
00:05:25,906 --> 00:05:29,458
If it is still wrong, the player ran out
of tries.

100
00:05:40,813 --> 00:05:41,373
Correct!

101
00:05:41,393 --> 00:05:42,025
Tries: 3.

102
00:05:42,045 --> 00:05:47,533
The third guess was right, so break
stopped the loop, and line 15 never ran.

103
00:05:48,117 --> 00:05:49,759
Step 3: a real secret.

104
00:05:49,779 --> 00:05:51,345
A fixed 7 is no game.

105
00:05:51,365 --> 00:05:54,291
Python has a module for random numbers.

106
00:05:54,311 --> 00:05:59,277
A module is a file of ready-made Python
code, and import loads it.

107
00:05:59,300 --> 00:06:04,764
Random dot rand int, 1 comma 20, picks a
whole number from 1 to 20.

108
00:06:04,784 --> 00:06:09,020
Both ends are included: 1 and 20 can both
be picked.

109
00:06:09,067 --> 00:06:11,318
The module also has rand range.

110
00:06:11,338 --> 00:06:16,155
It works like range from Chapter 8: it
stops before its end value.

111
00:06:16,175 --> 00:06:19,087
So rand range 1 comma 20 never picks 20.

112
00:06:19,133 --> 00:06:23,366
And a seed makes the same random numbers
on every run.

113
00:06:23,386 --> 00:06:27,304
That sounds strange, but it is useful for
testing.

114
00:06:27,920 --> 00:06:30,270
Line 2 imports the module.

115
00:06:30,290 --> 00:06:32,640
Line 3 sets the seed to 3.

116
00:06:32,667 --> 00:06:36,917
Lines 4 to 6 pick three numbers: 8, 19 and
18.

117
00:06:36,967 --> 00:06:41,115
On the same Python, the same numbers come
back on every run.

118
00:06:41,135 --> 00:06:44,102
Your numbers may differ on another
version.

119
00:06:44,122 --> 00:06:47,645
Delete line 3, and the numbers change on
every run.

120
00:06:47,665 --> 00:06:49,313
A real game has no seed.

121
00:06:49,920 --> 00:06:51,317
Stage 3: generate.

122
00:06:51,337 --> 00:06:56,591
Asha asks the AI for the secret line, from
1 to 20, including both.

123
00:06:56,611 --> 00:07:00,290
And she asks exactly which numbers it can
pick.

124
00:07:00,333 --> 00:07:04,166
The AI writes one line with rand range, 1
comma 20.

125
00:07:04,186 --> 00:07:08,093
And it says every number from 1 to 20 can
be picked.

126
00:07:08,113 --> 00:07:09,226
It sounds sure.

127
00:07:20,580 --> 00:07:20,786
No.

128
00:07:20,806 --> 00:07:24,555
Rand range stops before its end value,
like range.

129
00:07:24,575 --> 00:07:26,364
But Asha does not argue.

130
00:07:26,384 --> 00:07:27,344
She tests it.

131
00:07:27,953 --> 00:07:30,947
Line 6 is a for loop from Chapter 8.

132
00:07:30,967 --> 00:07:35,133
It runs line 7, the AI's line, ten
thousand times.

133
00:07:35,167 --> 00:07:40,543
Lines 4 and 5 start lowest at 20 and
highest at 1, the opposite ends, so the

134
00:07:40,563 --> 00:07:42,957
first number picked replaces both.

135
00:07:43,000 --> 00:07:47,469
Lines 8 to 11 keep the lowest and the
highest number so far.

136
00:07:47,489 --> 00:07:52,107
Line 3 is a seed, so the test gives the
same result every run.

137
00:08:03,480 --> 00:08:04,501
Highest, 20.

138
00:08:04,521 --> 00:08:05,975
But it prints 19!

139
00:08:05,995 --> 00:08:09,530
In ten thousand draws, 20 never appeared.

140
00:08:09,567 --> 00:08:13,023
A customer with a jar of 20 biscuits could
never win.

141
00:08:13,620 --> 00:08:14,759
Stage 4: test.

142
00:08:14,779 --> 00:08:19,310
Asha shows the AI the line and the result:
highest, 19.

143
00:08:19,333 --> 00:08:20,998
Now the AI agrees.

144
00:08:21,018 --> 00:08:23,525
Rand range stops before 20.

145
00:08:23,545 --> 00:08:26,613
It suggests rand int, 1 comma 20.

146
00:08:26,633 --> 00:08:29,858
Asha runs the same test with rand int.

147
00:08:29,878 --> 00:08:30,883
Highest, 20.

148
00:08:30,903 --> 00:08:32,334
Both ends appear.

149
00:08:32,953 --> 00:08:33,716
Quick check.

150
00:08:33,736 --> 00:08:37,434
Which line picks a whole number from 1 to
6, like a dice?

151
00:08:48,813 --> 00:08:48,972
B.

152
00:08:48,992 --> 00:08:50,760
Rand int, 1 comma 6.

153
00:08:50,780 --> 00:08:52,815
Both ends are included.

154
00:08:52,835 --> 00:08:55,675
Rand range would never roll a 6.

155
00:08:56,287 --> 00:08:56,805
Breather.

156
00:08:56,825 --> 00:09:00,757
The secret is random now, and you know
every number can be picked.

157
00:09:00,800 --> 00:09:03,927
Next, the crash from the start of this
video.

158
00:09:08,650 --> 00:09:09,653
Stage 5: debug.

159
00:09:09,673 --> 00:09:14,702
Asha pastes the two lines and the full
error from the start of this video.

160
00:09:14,722 --> 00:09:18,250
She asks for the cause first, then the
smallest fix.

161
00:09:18,300 --> 00:09:19,810
The AI names line 3.

162
00:09:19,830 --> 00:09:22,412
Int cannot convert the word seven.

163
00:09:22,432 --> 00:09:28,150
The fix: keep the answer as text, check it
first, and only then convert it.

164
00:09:28,753 --> 00:09:30,015
Step 4: the check.

165
00:09:30,035 --> 00:09:35,996
The string method is digit gives True only
when the text has at least one character,

166
00:09:36,016 --> 00:09:38,203
and every character is a digit.

167
00:09:38,233 --> 00:09:42,469
So no letters, no minus sign, no decimal
point, and no spaces.

168
00:09:42,489 --> 00:09:45,283
An empty answer has no characters at all.

169
00:09:45,333 --> 00:09:50,285
Line 8 removes the space with strip from
Chapter 3 first, then checks.

170
00:10:01,647 --> 00:10:02,374
Only two.

171
00:10:02,394 --> 00:10:04,033
Line 2, the text 12.

172
00:10:04,053 --> 00:10:08,017
And line 8, where strip removed the space
first.

173
00:10:08,067 --> 00:10:13,070
Line 7 is the surprise: a space before the
7 is enough to give False.

174
00:10:13,650 --> 00:10:15,872
Step 5 puts the check in order.

175
00:10:15,892 --> 00:10:19,198
Line 2 reads the answer and strips the
spaces.

176
00:10:19,218 --> 00:10:20,210
It stays text.

177
00:10:20,233 --> 00:10:24,963
Line 3: if it is not all digits, line 4
asks for a whole number.

178
00:10:25,000 --> 00:10:28,653
Line 5: if it is outside 1 to 20, line 6
says so.

179
00:10:28,673 --> 00:10:34,875
Line 5 can safely call int, because line 3
already found that the answer is made of

180
00:10:34,895 --> 00:10:35,400
digits.

181
00:10:35,433 --> 00:10:39,579
Only a fair answer reaches the else, where
line 8 converts it.

182
00:10:50,947 --> 00:10:53,714
Line 4 prints: please type a whole number.

183
00:10:53,734 --> 00:10:56,900
And int never sees the word, so nothing
crashes.

184
00:10:57,483 --> 00:10:59,406
Step 6: the complete game.

185
00:10:59,426 --> 00:11:05,460
Lines 2 to 5 set it up: the module, the
header with the rules, the secret and the

186
00:11:05,480 --> 00:11:06,058
counter.

187
00:11:06,078 --> 00:11:10,541
In the real game, line 4 is random dot
rand int, 1 comma 20.

188
00:11:10,561 --> 00:11:14,203
Here it is fixed at 7, so you can follow
one run.

189
00:11:14,233 --> 00:11:16,928
Line 6 is the loop from step 2.

190
00:11:16,948 --> 00:11:19,993
Line 7 reads and strips the answer.

191
00:11:20,033 --> 00:11:23,403
Lines 8 to 11 are the checks from step 5.

192
00:11:23,433 --> 00:11:29,263
Only a fair guess reaches line 14, so only
a fair guess counts as a try.

193
00:11:29,300 --> 00:11:36,420
Lines 15 to 21 are the win and the hints
from step 2, and lines 22 and 23 run after

194
00:11:36,440 --> 00:11:37,194
the loop.

195
00:11:48,547 --> 00:11:48,777
No.

196
00:11:48,797 --> 00:11:51,859
0 is all digits, so it passes line 8.

197
00:11:51,879 --> 00:11:56,274
But it is less than 1, so line 11 prints
the message.

198
00:11:56,294 --> 00:12:00,106
Line 14 never runs, so the try is not
counted.

199
00:12:00,720 --> 00:12:01,344
Breather.

200
00:12:01,364 --> 00:12:02,561
Relax your hands.

201
00:12:02,581 --> 00:12:03,850
The game is built.

202
00:12:03,900 --> 00:12:06,377
But criterion five says: test it.

203
00:12:06,397 --> 00:12:09,327
And a random secret makes testing hard.

204
00:12:14,050 --> 00:12:16,108
How do you test a random game?

205
00:12:16,128 --> 00:12:20,681
If the secret changes every run, you
cannot know the right result.

206
00:12:20,701 --> 00:12:26,501
So each test is a copy of the game, where
line 4 sets a fixed secret, such as 7, and

207
00:12:26,521 --> 00:12:28,302
has its own typed answers.

208
00:12:28,920 --> 00:12:32,710
Asha writes six tests, each with the
result she expects.

209
00:12:32,733 --> 00:12:34,423
A win on the first try.

210
00:12:34,467 --> 00:12:37,407
The high, low, win game you saw at the
start.

211
00:12:37,433 --> 00:12:42,413
Words and an empty answer, which must not
count as tries.

212
00:12:42,433 --> 00:12:45,873
Five wrong guesses, to reach the end of
the tries.

213
00:12:45,900 --> 00:12:47,320
And the boundary values.

214
00:12:47,340 --> 00:12:51,280
The largest secret, 20, with the answers 0
and 21 outside the jar.

215
00:12:51,300 --> 00:12:53,132
And the smallest secret, 1.

216
00:12:53,753 --> 00:12:54,277
Test 3.

217
00:12:54,297 --> 00:12:55,520
The secret is 7.

218
00:12:55,540 --> 00:13:00,645
The player presses enter with no answer,
then types seven, then 7.

219
00:13:12,013 --> 00:13:12,285
One.

220
00:13:12,305 --> 00:13:17,617
The empty answer and the word each got a
message, and only the 7 counted.

221
00:13:17,637 --> 00:13:17,982
Pass!

222
00:13:18,587 --> 00:13:19,183
Test 4.

223
00:13:19,203 --> 00:13:24,199
The secret is 7, and the player guesses 1,
2, 3, 4 and 5.

224
00:13:35,580 --> 00:13:36,513
Out of tries.

225
00:13:36,533 --> 00:13:37,173
It was 7.

226
00:13:37,193 --> 00:13:42,820
The tries left counted down to 0, and the
loop stopped after the fifth guess.

227
00:13:42,840 --> 00:13:43,187
Pass!

228
00:13:43,787 --> 00:13:44,327
Test 5.

229
00:13:44,347 --> 00:13:45,687
The secret is 20.

230
00:13:45,707 --> 00:13:50,567
Answers 0 and 21 are outside the jar, so
each gets a message.

231
00:13:50,600 --> 00:13:53,543
Remember the question about 0 in step 6?

232
00:13:53,563 --> 00:13:58,210
Here is the proof: 0 got a message, and
did not count as a try.

233
00:13:58,233 --> 00:13:58,992
Then 20.

234
00:13:59,012 --> 00:14:01,814
Correct, with one guess used.

235
00:14:01,834 --> 00:14:02,300
Pass!

236
00:14:02,920 --> 00:14:03,453
Test 6.

237
00:14:03,473 --> 00:14:06,773
The secret is 1, and the player guesses 1.

238
00:14:06,793 --> 00:14:07,405
Correct.

239
00:14:07,425 --> 00:14:07,800
Pass!

240
00:14:07,820 --> 00:14:11,199
All six tests match their expected
results.

241
00:14:11,783 --> 00:14:13,001
Stage 6: improve.

242
00:14:13,021 --> 00:14:14,384
All six tests pass.

243
00:14:14,404 --> 00:14:20,063
Asha asks for a readability review, and
says: do not change what it prints, or

244
00:14:20,083 --> 00:14:21,883
which answers it accepts.

245
00:14:21,933 --> 00:14:25,280
The AI suggests removing strip from line
7.

246
00:14:25,300 --> 00:14:28,412
It says is digit already ignores spaces.

247
00:14:39,780 --> 00:14:39,998
No.

248
00:14:40,018 --> 00:14:43,406
In step 4, a space before the 7 gave
False.

249
00:14:43,426 --> 00:14:49,826
Asha tests the suggestion anyway, with an
easy typing slip: 7, with a space after

250
00:14:49,846 --> 00:14:50,064
it.

251
00:14:50,687 --> 00:14:52,503
Line 7 has no strip now.

252
00:14:52,523 --> 00:14:55,717
The player types 7, with a space after it.

253
00:14:55,767 --> 00:14:57,614
Please type a whole number!

254
00:14:57,634 --> 00:15:01,557
The answer looks exactly like 7, and the
game rejects it.

255
00:15:01,600 --> 00:15:06,848
The suggestion changed which answers the
game accepts, and Asha said not to.

256
00:15:06,868 --> 00:15:07,957
She keeps strip.

257
00:15:08,553 --> 00:15:11,223
Here are all six stages on one game.

258
00:15:11,267 --> 00:15:13,337
The AI helped at every stage.

259
00:15:13,367 --> 00:15:18,077
And it was confidently wrong twice: the
secret line, and the spaces.

260
00:15:18,100 --> 00:15:21,573
Both times, a test with a known result
caught it.

261
00:15:22,187 --> 00:15:22,860
Quick check.

262
00:15:22,880 --> 00:15:25,516
How do you test a game whose secret is
random?

263
00:15:36,880 --> 00:15:37,008
A.

264
00:15:37,028 --> 00:15:41,954
Fix the secret in a test copy, then run
answers with known results.

265
00:15:41,974 --> 00:15:43,578
Exactly what Asha did.

266
00:15:44,187 --> 00:15:45,041
Last breather.

267
00:15:45,061 --> 00:15:49,477
You built a game that handles every
answer, and you made the decisions.

268
00:15:49,500 --> 00:15:50,613
Let's recap.

269
00:15:55,350 --> 00:15:57,700
Here is what you used in this boss level.

270
00:15:57,733 --> 00:16:01,753
Import loads a module, and rand int
includes both ends.

271
00:16:01,800 --> 00:16:05,210
Rand range stops before its end value,
like range.

272
00:16:05,233 --> 00:16:07,546
A seed repeats the same numbers.

273
00:16:07,566 --> 00:16:10,243
Use it for tests, not for real games.

274
00:16:10,267 --> 00:16:15,667
Check, then convert: strip, is digit, a
range check, and only then int.

275
00:16:15,700 --> 00:16:19,201
And test a random program by fixing its
random part.

276
00:16:19,820 --> 00:16:20,957
One last check.

277
00:16:20,977 --> 00:16:24,120
A player types 15, with spaces around it.

278
00:16:24,140 --> 00:16:25,354
The secret is 9.

279
00:16:25,374 --> 00:16:27,282
What does the game print?

280
00:16:38,647 --> 00:16:38,783
C.

281
00:16:38,803 --> 00:16:42,758
Strip removes the spaces, so 15 passes
both checks.

282
00:16:42,778 --> 00:16:46,812
It is the first fair guess, and it is
higher than 9.

283
00:16:46,832 --> 00:16:48,839
Too high, four tries left.

284
00:16:49,453 --> 00:16:51,006
Boss level complete!

285
00:16:51,026 --> 00:16:54,389
You earned 300 XP and the Game Maker
badge.

286
00:16:54,409 --> 00:16:56,040
And Module 2 is done.

287
00:16:58,153 --> 00:17:02,713
The practice file has eight more tasks,
worth 170 XP.

288
00:17:02,733 --> 00:17:06,663
Three warm-ups, worth 10 XP each,
including two dice.

289
00:17:06,700 --> 00:17:10,650
Three apply tasks, worth 20 each, such as
three tries for a PIN.

290
00:17:10,700 --> 00:17:13,365
And two stretch tasks, worth 40 each.

291
00:17:13,385 --> 00:17:19,025
In the last one, you fix an AI's game by
counting how many times it asks for a

292
00:17:19,045 --> 00:17:19,460
guess.

293
00:17:19,500 --> 00:17:22,837
Write down the expected result before you
run each one.

294
00:17:23,417 --> 00:17:27,348
Asha's game forgets each guess as soon as
the next one arrives.

295
00:17:27,368 --> 00:17:30,797
At the end, it cannot show the player all
five guesses.

296
00:17:30,833 --> 00:17:36,370
In Chapter 11, you will learn lists: one
variable that keeps many values in order.

297
00:17:36,390 --> 00:17:39,013
Then the game can remember every guess.

298
00:17:39,033 --> 00:17:40,820
Do the warm-up tasks now.

299
00:17:40,840 --> 00:17:42,843
They take about ten minutes.

300
00:17:42,867 --> 00:17:44,708
See you in Chapter 11.
